00:01:05.126 --> 00:01:06.750
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now,
is that run time?

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.232
Or is that at build
or developer time?

00:01:10.232 --> 00:01:11.190
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.630
So we think about
container security

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.170
at Google in three
different aspects.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.350
One is what we'll call making
sure your containers are

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.160
secure to develop.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.460
So do I have some way of
managing my network policies,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.240
and secrets, and different
kinds of deployment policies,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.310
identities, et cetera
that I'm going to have.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.980
The second area is what
we'll call build and deploy

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:29.540
securities.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.220
So how do you know that your
images are vulnerability-free,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.179
don't contain
0-days, that they've

00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:35.970
gone through your CICD
pipeline before they

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:36.780
deploy to production.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.690
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:38.880
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: The main
thing that we have in that space

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.090
right now is something
called Grafeas.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.479
It's an open source project.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:43.770
There's a couple talks,
actually, at KubeCon on Grafeas

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.260
by the TL of that
project, Wendy.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.800
And that allows you to track
the metadata of your projects,

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.840
including vulnerability scan
results for those images

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.510
so you can then
determine whether or not

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.940
you can deploy those images.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.000
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Kubernetes at large,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.749
it seems like there's
a lot of work that's

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:01.622
gone into more secure
images without zero days.

00:02:01.622 --> 00:02:03.330
It seems like something
that we've really

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.840
got to get on top of, right?

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:05.850
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
It's certainly an area

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.260
with a lot of low-hanging fruit.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.660
And there's a lot of
customer and partner

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.610
interest in that space.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.972
The main problem to
solve in that space right

00:02:13.972 --> 00:02:15.930
now is just the sheer
volume of vulnerabilities

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.920
that people have.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.664
So I've had customers
ask me, what

00:02:18.664 --> 00:02:20.580
do I do if I find a
vulnerability that doesn't

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.230
have a patch that exists yet?

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.600
And the reality
is, you're probably

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.020
thinking too far ahead.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.090
You probably have a
ton of vulnerabilities

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.170
that are completely unpatched.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:28.400
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.775
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Fix those first

00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:32.430
before you start to worry about
things that are unpatchable.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.020
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
is the best advice

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.990
we have to give is rely on
the operating systems vendors

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.150
to provide those updates,
and just make sure

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:43.170
that those updates are as
installed as possible in all

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.730
of your base images?

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:45.270
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
So you're thinking

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.070
of actually a different layer
than what I'm thinking of.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:47.903
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:02:47.903 --> 00:02:50.060
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So you
can talk about the node OS

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.170
and the container OS.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.970
And Google uses
container optimized OS

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.770
as the node OS, which
was open source.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.300
It's based on Chromium OS.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.950
And we have a great
blog post as to why

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.890
we think that's built
in a really secure way.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.544
And then you can use
the container OS.

00:03:04.544 --> 00:03:06.460
And we have lots of
options, again, on Google.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.310
Or you can bring your own.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.280
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.600
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And then
we have the image itself

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:10.230
that you're deploying.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.700
And that might itself
have vulnerabilities.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:12.000
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.700
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So if you're
running NGINX, or WordPress,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.132
and that's a two-year-old
image, maybe you want to patch

00:03:18.132 --> 00:03:19.090
that before you deploy.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.860
Maybe you want to check
the age of your images.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.470
Maybe you want to run some
standard scans, et cetera,

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.520
before you deploy that.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.978
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And which of those

00:03:24.978 --> 00:03:26.850
is the user's
responsibility, versus Google

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.682
takes care of on your behalf?

00:03:28.682 --> 00:03:29.640
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.320
If you're using a node
OS or a container OS,

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.480
it's provided by Google.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:34.780
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.270
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It's Google's
responsibly to patch it.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.290
But it's your responsibility
to pick what you want.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:38.720
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.730
So the user picks.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:40.100
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Correct.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:40.800
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
then Google handles

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.890
the patching of the base OS as
a managed service effectively.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.300
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: If they
pick an OS that we provide.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:45.630
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.046
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
So if you choose

00:03:47.046 --> 00:03:48.840
to bring your own
container OS, then it's

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.490
your responsibility, obviously.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:54.637
The image itself is your
responsibility to secure.

00:03:54.637 --> 00:03:55.470
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.844
But this single
pane of glass now

00:03:56.844 --> 00:03:58.485
should maybe help
users a bit more?

00:03:58.485 --> 00:03:59.360
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Ah.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.450
So that's the third area.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.130
So that's our container
runtime security

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.110
area, which is, I have
a container now running

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.370
in production.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.340
And I've done everything
I can to fiddle

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.910
with all the things in GKE, and
scan my images before they're

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:12.810
deployed.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.490
But what if there
still is an 0-day,

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:15.870
and what if somebody
gets into my container

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.310
and starts doing something bad?

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.587
How do I detect that,
react, do forensics

00:04:20.587 --> 00:04:21.670
after the fact, et cetera?

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:22.200
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.560
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And that's
container runtime security.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:23.750
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.583
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
that's a super nascent

00:04:25.583 --> 00:04:28.200
space, an area we realize
there's a lot of interest in.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.920
Hence, the integrations of
five partners with the Cloud

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:31.920
Security Command Center.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:32.550
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.620
And are any of these
helping us with forensics?

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.578
I'm guessing that's
snapshotting the container,

00:04:36.578 --> 00:04:39.460
knowing what it looked like
at various points in time,

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.360
maybe being able to roll
back debug logs or monitoring

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.910
logs, and--

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.100
I don't know-- trace the
origination of the attack?

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.482
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: That's a
still fairly new capability.

00:04:49.482 --> 00:04:50.315
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:04:50.315 --> 00:04:51.210
Wishful thinking, right?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.168
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah.

00:04:52.168 --> 00:04:54.890
A lot of the partners
have tooling around

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:55.884
a longer set of logs.

00:04:55.884 --> 00:04:58.050
So you might have logs that
are written temporarily,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.799
to like a ring
buffer, for example.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.340
And then once you
detect an incident,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.860
you flag those logs and
start writing those.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:03.819
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:05:03.819 --> 00:05:05.859
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It's
not necessarily full image

00:05:05.859 --> 00:05:07.280
snapshotting that's
happening yet.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.238
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:05:08.238 --> 00:05:10.160
That's where we're at today.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.460
So we covered the
three different areas.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.860
I'm curious if there
are kinds of attacks

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.450
that you'd like to
maybe highlight?

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.970
The types of things
that either developers

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.160
should be aware of when
writing an application,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.590
or IT administrators
should be aware

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.570
of as their operating clusters?

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:26.810
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:27.950
I mean, it's, in
some sense, it's kind

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.480
of boring for a
security professional.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.520
It's not that different than
what you see as attacking a VM,

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.019
right?

00:05:32.019 --> 00:05:36.170
You're going to see credential
theft, privilege escalation.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.910
You might see abuse of compute.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:43.060
In this case, crypto mining's
a fairly popular thing to do.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.026
They're not new
kinds of attacks.

00:05:45.026 --> 00:05:46.400
And often people
are actually not

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.620
aware that they're
in a container

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.370
when they're
[INAUDIBLE] attacks.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.110
They're not attacking
Kubernetes per se.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.570
They're just attacking
some open compute thing.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:55.189
And oh, I happen to
be in a container.

00:05:55.189 --> 00:05:55.730
That's great.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:56.730
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Indeed.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.370
Interesting.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.390
So different question.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.300
Moving away from
security a little bit.

